R"VG(
# sunlost11.vg (D-05): genre.daycount, continued (its first part in sunlost6.vg: a file holds at most 16 KB)
@apart

dc.cl.first
  Day {#dc.dayn} without the dawn. {dc.cl.body}
  The {#dc.dayn:ord} day. {dc.cl.body}
  Day {#dc.dayn}. {dc.cl.body}
dc.cl.next
  The next day. {dc.cl.body}
  {n} days later. {dc.cl.body}
  Some days on. {dc.cl.body}
  A {dc.span} after. {dc.cl.body}
  (has:festival) On the day of {festival}, kept below without the sun it was for. {dc.cl.body}
dc.cl.body (share=0.75)
  (once) The fire was kept. {dc.cl.what}
  (once) {dc.cl.what}
  (once) Nothing to set down but the count, and [the cold at the door|the dark on the stair|the frost on the stones|the wind that is not there].
  (once) The ice was cut above and carried down; the water is {dc.cl.water}.
  (once) Someone went up and looked; the stars stood where the sun had been.
  (once) {dc.cl.death}
  (once) The old count was full, and [this book begun|a new one begun by the youngest keeper|the old book carried up to the dead].
  (once) {dc.cl.born}
  (once) The keepers of the count disagreed by a day, and it was settled [by lot|by the eldest|by {ruler.the}].
  (once) The {dark.food} came up well by {warm.ground}. {dc.cl.what}
  (once) A day like the one before it, [and the one before that|and like the one to come|but colder at the stair].
dc.cl.what (people=5)
  The {dark.food} grew.
  Two children were born, and named for the warm stones.
  The last of the old {fuel} was opened; there is little left.
  The last {herd} were killed and shared out.
  A song was made about {sun}; nobody sang it twice.
  The water from the ice was sweet.
  The air was bad by the north wall, and the sleepers there were moved.
  The young asked again what the dawn was like.
  Somebody painted a sun on the wall of {hall.below}, and the keepers let it stay.
  The old ones sat by {warm.ground} and said the names of the summers.
  (has:festival) We sang the songs of {festival}, low, so as not to wake the children.
dc.cl.water (people=2)
  clean
  bitter
  sweet
  grey with ash
dc.cl.death (people=3)
  The oldest of us died, and was carried up into the cold, where the dead keep.
  A child died of the cough. The cough is in every hall.
  Two were lost above, cutting ice; the cold took them before they could come down.
  The keeper of the fire died at the fire.
dc.cl.born (people=3)
  A child was born below, who will never see the sun; we named it for {sun}.
  Twins were born by {warm.ground}, and both lived.
  A child was born, the first this year, and we said the names over it.
  A child was born, and its mother asked what it would be called in the old words for daylight.

dc.cl.close (formula people=3)
  So many days, and no dawn among them.
  The count goes on; may the one who keeps it after us not lose it.
  {god.keep}, and keep the count.
  Let whoever finds this go on counting.
  Count on. One day there will be a dawn to stop for.

# =====================================================================================================================
# a printed count, posted in the halls
# =====================================================================================================================

dc.pr.head (formula people=3)
  THE COUNT. Day {#dc.dayn} without the dawn.
  The days since the last sunrise: {#dc.dayn}. Posted at {$home}.
  {$home}, below. Day {#dc.dayn} of the count.
  The count of the days, as kept at {$seat}: today the {#dc.dayn:ord}.
dc.pr.line (share=0.8)
  (once) The {drink} ration for {festival} is one {vessel.one.n} a household; the count is kept as usual.
  (once) {pastime} is permitted in the lower hall on the rest day, the count being read first.
  (once) The {herd} on the warm level number {n}; the {dark.food} stores are counted with them.
  (once) At the head of the shaft the frost is on everything, and the air outside will not come in.
  (once) The {dark.food} beds by {warm.ground} are to be turned this week.
  (once) The count was checked against [the clock at {$seat}|the old almanac|the keepers' own count]; it stands.
  (once) Births this month: {n}. Deaths: {n}. The count goes on.
  (once) The old calendar's {festival} falls in {n} days; it will be kept in the lower hall.
  (once) The doors to the surface are shut except for the ice parties.
  (once) The sky party went up and reports the stars as they were; {sun} is no brighter.
  (once) The warmth at {warm.ground} is [steady|failing on the east side|better since the new shaft]. The upper rooms are to be closed.
  (once) Lamps are to be turned down [after the evening count|at the second bell|on every level but the infirmary].
  (once) {sunway.thing} is still seen from the surface, the sky party says, but it is going too.
dc.pr.end (formula people=3)
  The count is kept by the office at {$seat}; corrections to the office.
  Next count posted in ten days.
  Keep the count. Keep the lamps low.
  By order, at {$seat}.

# =====================================================================================================================
# a reel's or a drive's log (the modern and the futuristic ages)
# =====================================================================================================================

dc.lg.head (formula people=4)
  Count log, {$home}. Day {#dc.dayn} since the last sunrise.
  (has:$me) This is {$me:name}, keeping the count at {$home}. Day {#dc.dayn}.
  (age>=futuristic) Cycle {#year:digits}. Day {#dc.dayn} of the dark.
  The count, {$home}, below. Day {#dc.dayn}.
  (has:$me) Day {#dc.dayn}. {$me:name} on the count tonight.
dc.lg.line (share=0.65)
  (once) Outside: the frost, the stars, nothing [moving|living|changed].
  (once) Inside: warm enough by {warm.ground}, cold [everywhere else|on the upper levels|at the doors].
  (once) Heat from the deep rock [steady|down a little|up after the new shaft]. Air [good on the lower levels|thin by the north stair|bad again in the old rooms].
  (once) The {dc.party} went up for ice[. All back|, {n} of them; {n} came down|. One frostbitten hand, nothing worse].
  (once) [Two|Three] of the old ones died this [week|month]. They went up with the {dc.party}.
  (once) Birth on the [lower|warm|third] level. [Healthy|Small, but breathing|Twins]. The count goes on.
  (once) The {dark.food} came in [well|thin|late]. Rations [as before|cut by a quarter|as before, for now].
  (once) [The children asked about the sun again|A child asked what morning meant|The young ones wanted to hear about the sun]. [Told them what we remember|Nobody could agree what colour it was|The old ones told it, as always].
  (once) The lights [flickered for an hour|failed on the fifth level|were dimmed to save the line]. [Nobody slept|We sat in the dark and counted|The children sang].
  (once) {sunway.thing} is no longer seen from the surface; the sky party [could not find it|looked for an hour|says it is gone].
  (once) [The stars at noon, as every noon|Noon, and the stars|The noon bell, and the same stars]. [The surface party swore it was colder|Nobody looks up any more|The old ones still look up].
  (once) [The frost line|The ice in the shaft|The cold at the stair] has come down [{n}|another {n}] steps since the last count.
  (once) A [quarrel|fight] at the warm rock over [places|a blanket|the shift]. Settled by the wardens.
  (once) Water from the melt tanks [is clean|tastes of the rock|is short this week], one {vessel.one.n} a head.
  (once) The {herd} that were left were [killed and shared|moved to the warm level|counted again: {n}].
  (once) Someone painted {sun} on the wall of the {dc.lg.room}. It is still there.
  (once) [Music|A dance|A story] in the {dc.lg.room} tonight. Most came.
  (once) {pastime} in the {dc.lg.room} tonight; [the young ones won|the old ones won|nobody kept the score].
  (once) The last of the {drink} was opened for {festival}. [Nobody said so, but it was the last|It went round twice|The children had the dregs].
  (once) The {herd} are down to {n}. [Kept at the warm rock|Fed before we eat|Nobody will say what happens after].
  (once) Someone played {instrument} in the {dc.lg.room}. [Most came to listen|The old ones cried|It was out of tune and nobody minded].
  (once) The {craft} rooms open again on the lower level. [Work for idle hands|The first thing made was for a child|{n} came the first day].
  (once age>=futuristic) The halls' mind keeps its own count; ours and its agree [to the day|to the hour|again].
  (once age>=modern inv.radio=1) The transmitter was tried again[. Nothing came back|, on the long band. Static|. The other halls did not answer].
dc.lg.room (people=2)
  main hall
  lower hall
  dining level
  old store
dc.lg.end (formula people=3)
  End of log.
  Count continues.
  Logging off. Day {#dc.dayn}.
  That's the count for today.

# =====================================================================================================================
# a crystal's reckoning (the futuristic age's and the far future's)
# =====================================================================================================================

dc.cr.head (formula people=4)
  The count, as {$home} keeps it: {#darkyears|year} of the dark.
  The reckoning of the dark, from the last sunrise to this reading: day {#dc.dayn}.
  Day {#dc.dayn} of the dark. The count, kept in the crystal so that it cannot be lost.
  (age=farfuture) The count of the days below, which the {people} keep as they keep everything: exactly.
  (age=futuristic) The keeping count of {$home}: day {#dc.dayn} since the sun went.
dc.cr.line (share=0.7)
  (once) Every day is counted, though no day has a dawn to begin it.
  (once) The warm levels hold. The cold levels are [shut and counted too|sealed, and their doors counted|empty, and kept].
  (once) The {dark.food} are counted with the days, and the water, and the air.
  (once) So many years since the last sunrise that the children of the dark now outnumber those who saw it.
  (once) The count is read aloud at {festival}, and everyone listens to the number grow.
  (once) The star that was the sun is still marked on the ceiling of the [hall|great hall|lower hall], where it was last seen.
  (once) Nothing failed today. That is also counted.
  (once) The halls are quiet. The count is the loudest thing in them.
  (once) [Births|New names] since the last reading: {n}. [Deaths|Names gone up]: {n}.
  (once) The heat of the rock is down by a [hair|breath|little] since the count began, and counted.
  (once) The surface was looked at [today|this cycle|again]: the frost, the stars, no change.
  (once) The [old ones|eldest] say the summers in their sleep, and the count takes them down too.
  (once) The {craft} goes on in the warm levels, and its makers count the days by what they finish.
  (once) {feast.custom} was kept below on its day, and the count read out before it.
  (once age=farfuture) The count has outlasted the counters, as it was made to.
  (once age=farfuture) We count the days because the dark does not; it has no days.
  (once age=farfuture) The count is older than anyone who keeps it, and younger than the {people}.
dc.cr.end (formula people=2)
  The count goes on.
  Read the number aloud; it is the {people}'s.
  End of the reckoning, for today.

# =====================================================================================================================
# the far future's hum (the machine renders what it can)
# =====================================================================================================================

dc.hm.head (formula people=3)
  The hum counts. The machine renders each pulse as one of their days: {#dc.dayn} so far.
  A slow hum, one rise for every day since the sun went; the machine counts {#dc.dayn}.
  The record hums in groups of {dc.group}. The machine reads it as a count of the days of the dark.
dc.hm.line
  (once) The pulses never miss.
  (once) Every so often the hum drops a tone, the way a voice drops at a name.
  (once) A longer pulse marks each of their years, and the pulses between are their days.
  (once) Under the count, very faint, a tone the machine has heard before: the {people}'s sound for the sun.
  (once) The hum is warm where the count was kept by {warm.ground}, the machine says, though it cannot say how it knows.
  (once) The rises do not slow toward the end; whoever kept this expected to keep it.
dc.hm.end
  Then the hum goes on, and the machine stops rendering it.
  The count does not end; the record does.
  The last pulse is still sounding when the record runs out.
)VG"
